Krishna AI: Foundational Knowledge Architecture for a Voice-First, Gita-Grounded Companion
TL;DR
The thesis holds: the LLM should be the last step. The strongest evidence base supports a "detection-first" pipeline in which 20–30 specialised engines infer appraisal, hidden emotion, defence mechanisms, internal Parts, and readiness before any Gita wisdom is retrieved — because appraisal theory (Scherer, Lazarus) shows emotion is downstream of interpretation, and empathy research (Sharma et al. 2020) shows that exploration and perceived understanding, not verse accuracy, drive engagement (stronger "explorations" earned 47% more replies from support seekers).
A four-level ontology of 500+ states is buildable today by fusing Shaver et al. (1987, 135 tertiary terms), Parrott (2001), Cowen & Keltner (2017, 27 gradient-linked categories), GoEmotions (Demszky et al. 2020, 27 labels), Scherer's Component Process Model appraisal dimensions, and Warriner et al. (2013) VAD norms for 13,915 words — cross-mapped to the Natyashastra's 49 bhavas (8 sthayi + 33 vyabhichari + 8 sattvika) and the Gita's Sanskrit emotion lexicon.
Cultural specificity and crisis safety are non-negotiable design constraints. South Asian distress is heavily somatised and spiritually framed, so the detection layer must read karma/dharma/detachment idioms as possible defences rather than face value; and crisis handling must never let the AI manage acute suicidality alone or apply spiritual bypassing.
Key Findings
Appraisal-first is empirically grounded. Scherer's Component Process Model decomposes emotion into four sequential Stimulus Evaluation Check groups — relevance (novelty, intrinsic pleasantness), implication (goal conduciveness, causal attribution), coping potential (control, power, adjustment), and normative significance (internal/external standards). Two people appraise the same event differently and therefore feel differently; the AI must infer appraisal, not surface affect. 
