Psychepedia
The taxonomy is composable, not off-the-shelf. No single framework reaches 500 states, but layering Shaver's 6→25→135 hierarchy, Parrott's tri-level tree, Cowen & Keltner's 27 categories, and quaternary micro-states (appraisal × temporal × relational nuance) yields the target. GoEmotions' 27 labels are the working NLP baseline but omit grief-vs-sadness nuance, sacred emotions, and existential states.
Hidden emotion is detectable through defence-mechanism linguistics. Vaillant's four-level hierarchy (psychotic, immature, neurotic, mature) maps onto surface-language fingerprints — intellectualisation shows high syntactic complexity and abstraction; minimisation shows hedging; somatisation shows body-state vocabulary. These are the bridge from "what is said" to "what is felt."
Internal Family Systems provides the multi-part decomposition. A single utterance contains Managers ("should/must"), Firefighters ("I just want it to stop"), Exiles (childlike/fragmented shame language), and Self (the 8 Cs: curiosity, calm, clarity, compassion, confidence, courage, creativity, connectedness). The "Inner Mahabharata" maps these to Gita characters.
Indian emotion theory is more granular than Western taxonomies for this product. The Natyashastra's 49 bhavas and the Gaudiya Vaishnava five-bhava rasa system (shanta, dasya, sakhya, vatsalya, madhurya) give a native vocabulary for spiritual emotion — especially the critical distinction between viraha (sacred separation, to be honoured) and vyatha (ordinary distress, to be soothed).
Emotional granularity is a trainable outcome, not just a measurement. Higher negative-emotion differentiation predicts more adaptive regulation, and it is reduced in depression — so a system that expands a user's emotional vocabulary over time is delivering a measurable, clinical-adjacent benefit and a defensible longitudinal KPI.
