PART 1 — Complete Emotion Science

1A. The four-level ontology.

Primary (valence/arousal clusters): built on Russell's circumplex and the Hourglass model.
Secondary (families): Shaver, Schwartz, Kirson & O'Connor (1987, JPSP 52(6):1061–1086, DOI 10.1037/0022-3514.52.6.1061) empirically derived 6 basic categories (love, joy, surprise, anger, sadness, fear) and 25 secondary categories via prototype/cluster analysis of similarity ratings.
Tertiary (135 terms): Shaver's full tertiary list (e.g., under Anger→Rage: anger, bitterness, dislike, ferocity, fury, hate, hostility, loathing, outrage, resentment, scorn, spite, vengefulness, wrath). Parrott (2001, Emotions in Social Psychology) reorganised these into a tree where secondary emotions derive from (not blend into) primaries — e.g., Love→Affection→{adoration, fondness, liking, attraction, caring, tenderness, compassion, sentimentality}; Love→Lust→{desire, passion, infatuation}; Love→Longing; Joy→Cheerfulness→{amusement, bliss, gaiety, glee, jolliness, joviality, delight, enjoyment, gladness, happiness, jubilation, elation, ecstasy, euphoria}; Joy→Zest→{enthusiasm, zeal, excitement, thrill, exhilaration}.
Quaternary (micro-states): compounds with appraisal + temporal + relational nuance ("bitterness from chronic injustice with no recourse" = anger + low coping potential + stable/global attribution + moral-norm violation; "wistful nostalgia tinged with regret for a road not taken"; "self-contempt masked as perfectionism").
Dimensional anchoring: every state gets VAD coordinates from Warriner, Kuperman & Brysbaert (2013, Behavior Research Methods 45:1191–1207, DOI 10.3758/s13428-012-0314-x), which provides valence/arousal/dominance ratings for 13,915 English lemmas on 1–9 scales (extending Bradley & Lang's 1,034-word ANEW).
Cross-cultural anchoring: Cowen & Keltner (2017, PNAS 114(38):E7900–E7909, DOI 10.1073/pnas.1702247114): "using statistical methods to analyze reports of emotional states elicited by 2,185 emotionally evocative short videos … we uncovered 27 varieties of reported emotional experience," rated by 800+ participants against 34 emotion categories and 14 affective dimensions. Categories are bridged by continuous gradients (awe↔peacefulness, horror↔sadness, amusement↔adoration), so the ontology should encode proximity, not hard boundaries. The GRID project (Fontaine, Scherer, Soriano) across 34 nations supplies cross-cultural emotion-semantic structure.

1B. Blends, transitions, dynamics. Emotion blends (jealousy = fear + anger + attachment insecurity + comparison), transitions driven by appraisal shifts, momentum vs. decay vs. loop, emotional inertia (linked to depression), meta-emotions (guilt about anger, shame about sadness, anxiety about anxiety), ambivalence (loving someone while wanting to leave; wanting success while fearing it), and emotional contagion — the last with direct implications for AI tone-matching in a voice interface.

1C. Emotional granularity (Barrett tradition) — the trainable metric. Barrett, Gross, Christensen & Benvenuto (2001, Cognition & Emotion 15(6):713–724, DOI 10.1080/02699930143000239) ran a 14-day diary study of 53 participants rating nine emotion-regulation strategies and found that "negative emotion differentiation was positively related to the frequency of negative emotion regulation, particularly at higher levels of emotional intensity." Kashdan, Barrett & McKnight (2015, Current Directions in Psychological Science 24(1):10–16, DOI 10.1177/0963721414550708) synthesised downstream effects: high differentiators consumed ~40% less alcohol under stress (Kashdan et al. 2010, Psych Science 21:1341–1347) and were 20–50% less likely to retaliate aggressively (Pond et al. 2012, Emotion 12:326–337), with reduced insula/ACC reactivity to rejection. Demiralp et al. (2012, Psychological Science 23(11):1410–1416, DOI 10.1177/0956797612444903) had participants rate four positive and seven negative emotions eight times daily for seven days and concluded: "individuals with MDD had less differentiated emotional experiences than did healthy participants, but only for negative emotions … above and beyond the effects of emotional intensity and variability." Design implication: granularity should be measured behaviourally via experience-sampling, not trait self-report — and increasing it is a core longitudinal KPI. Build a granularity-expansion table (15–25 sub-variants per basic emotion, e.g., for Sadness: wistful, bereaved, forlorn, crestfallen, heartbroken, disillusioned, disconnected, defeated, apathetic, grieving, desolate, mourning, melancholic, regretful, despondent) and grow it in-conversation by offering more precise labels the user can accept or refine.

1D. Appraisal theory deep dive. Integrate Lazarus (1991, Emotion and Adaptation; primary/secondary appraisal), Scherer (2001, multilevel sequential checking), Roseman (2013), and Smith & Ellsworth (1985). Detectable appraisal dimensions from language: certainty (hedging vs. absolutist words), agency (active vs. passive voice), fairness (moral-norm vocabulary), coping potential (control language), goal conduciveness, novelty. The core product insight: infer the appraisal that produced the emotion so the Gita verse reframes the appraisal, not merely soothes the affect.

1E. Moral, sacred, existential emotions.

Moral: shame vs. guilt is the pivotal linguistic distinction. Tangney & Dearing (2002, Shame and Guilt, Guilford): shame = global self-condemnation ("I am bad"); guilt = specific behaviour ("I did a bad thing"). Tangney, Wagner & Gramzow (1992, J. Abnormal Psychology 101(3):469–478, DOI 10.1037/0021-843X.101.3.469) found shame-proneness strongly tied to psychopathology (depression, anxiety) while guilt-proneness was largely unrelated once shame variance was partialled out. Shame follows internal-stable-global attributions (mirroring the depressive attributional style) and drives hiding/anger; guilt drives repair. This maps directly to identity-threat detection ("I failed" vs. "I am a failure") and to Gita 2.40 (no effort is wasted) and 18.66 (surrender releases self-condemnation). Add moral disgust, elevation, awe, gratitude, admiration, indignation, contempt, righteous anger, moral pride, embarrassment (Haidt, Prinz, Tangney).
Sacred/spiritual (first comprehensive taxonomy): bhakti (devotion), sharanagati (surrender), viraha (divine longing), sacred awe, transcendence, spiritual emptiness/dark night, faith, doubt, divine grace, reverence, religious scrupulosity/spiritual guilt, cosmic insignificance, numinous experience, spiritual bypassing, religious trauma — each with definition, triggers, linguistic presentation, distinction from secular equivalents, and Gita loci (Ch11 for numinous awe; 9.34 for divine longing).
Existential: Yalom (1980, Existential Psychotherapy) four ultimate concerns — death, freedom, isolation, meaninglessness — plus Frankl (1959, logotherapy), Heidegger, and Terror Management Theory (Greenberg, Solomon, Pyszczynski). These surface most at night; a primary use case for a voice companion. 
