2B. Indirect communication. Grice/Searle indirect speech acts: the Question Behind the Question ("What does the Gita say about forgiveness?" → hidden guilt or resentment; "How do I stop caring what others think?" → shame/social-evaluative anxiety), the minimiser-with-escalation, symptom-as-metaphor ("I can't sleep" = "I'm haunted"), the testing disclosure, the third-person buffer, the philosophical escape. Build a 50+ entry map of common general/philosophical queries to implied personal distress.

2C. Psycholinguistics. Pennebaker (2011, The Secret Life of Pronouns) and Tausczik & Pennebaker (2010): first-person-singular pronoun use rises with depression/distress but is better read as a marker of general negative emotionality (Tackman et al. 2018; meta-analytic r ≈ 0.13, Edwards & Holtzman); absolutist words mark depression/anxiety; high article/preposition use marks concrete/complex thinking; past-tense orientation marks rumination/trauma; future orientation marks anxiety; fragmented syntax marks overwhelm/dissociation; high sentence complexity marks intellectualisation. Add Eichstaedt et al. (2015) and Schwartz et al. (2013) for social-media-scale prediction. Document, for 200+ core states, the function words, content words, metaphor clusters, pronoun patterns, tense orientation, negation, hedging/certainty markers, voice (agency signal), and intensity qualifiers.

2D. Hidden-emotion prediction pipeline. Ten multi-task layers: (1) surface classification (25–30 categories, RoBERTa/BERT fine-tuned on GoEmotions as baseline — Demszky et al. 2020 report the BERT-base baseline "achieving 0.46 macro-F1 and 0.51 micro-F1 scores," with average Cohen's kappa of just 0.27 across categories); (2) fine-grained (200+ states); (3) intensity (1–10); (4) defence detection; (5) hidden-emotion inference (surface + defence + session history → probability distribution over abandonment fear, shame, grief, meaninglessness, worthlessness); (6) appraisal inference; (7) needs detection (Self-Determination Theory + safety/recognition/forgiveness/love/meaning/belonging); (8) identity-threat detection; (9) readiness (MI stages of change); (10) crisis detection with routing. Each layer names architecture, training-data source, key papers, and evaluation metric.

2E. Internal conflict / Parts. IFS (Schwartz 1995, 2001; Earley 2009, Self-Therapy; Anderson et al. 2017): Self + Managers + Firefighters + Exiles. Linguistic signals — Manager: "should/must/have to," planning, criticism, hypervigilance; Firefighter: "I just want to stop feeling this," avoidance/rage/addictive impulse; Exile: fragmented, childlike, small, shame/longing; Self: "I wonder," "I can see both sides," calm curiosity. The 8 Cs indicate Self-presence; detect the suppressed Part as much as the dominant one, because Parts polarise. Worked example — "I want to quit" contains a part wanting freedom, a part needing security, a part fearing disappointing parents, an exhausted part, and a part that still loves the work. Inner Mahabharata Framework: Arjuna = wounded Exile; Krishna = Self; Dhritarashtra = blind Manager; Kauravas = dysfunctional Firefighters; Pandavas = positive Parts (Yudhishthira = wisdom, Bhima = strength, Nakula/Sahadeva = skill/discernment); Draupadi = the ignored Exile demanding to be seen. Detection algorithm identifies which character is "speaking" and which verse Krishna offered that character; multi-part response templates acknowledge the conflict before offering wisdom, without taking sides or bypassing with a verse too soon.

PART 3 — Cognitive Architecture (the engine stack)

Twenty core engines run before the LLM: Conversation Analyzer → Emotion Analyzer (surface→hidden→blended) → Appraisal Analyzer → Need Detector → Defence Detector → Identity Analyzer → Attachment Analyzer (secure/avoidant/anxious/disorganised from language) → Values Analyzer → Belief/Distortion Analyzer → Meaning Analyzer → Life-Stage Analyzer (Erikson) → Spiritual-State Analyzer (Shraddha–Prema; faith, doubt, bypassing, genuine seeking) → Readiness Analyzer → Conflict Modeller → Narrative Analyzer (hero, victim, caretaker, warrior, seeker, lost child, perfectionist, martyr, provider, explorer) → Emotional Loop Detector (failure→self-criticism→overwork→burnout→failure) → Gita Mapper → Response Planner (validate, challenge, silence, question, verse, story, or action) → Bhav Detector (viraha vs. vyatha; nirveda vs. depression) → Voice Generator (tone, pacing, silence). Each engine's output is a typed feature vector feeding the next; the Gita Mapper and Response Planner consume the full state before the LLM renders language.

The 500-dimension user model ("Soul Graph") spans: Emotional (current state, baseline, volatility, granularity level, predominant family, recurring loops, regulation strategy); Psychological (self-esteem, self-compassion, core beliefs, active distortions, defence profile, attachment style, identity stability, locus of control, shame-vs-guilt profile, alexithymia); Motivational (purpose clarity, career/dharma alignment, intrinsic:extrinsic ratio, autonomy/competence/relatedness, service orientation); Relational; Spiritual (devotional stage Shraddha→Nishtha→Ruchi→Rati→Prema, faith 0–10, doubt profile, practice type/frequency/depth, bhav inclination, religious guilt/trauma); Existential (meaning, mortality awareness, freedom anxiety, isolation, cosmic belonging); Guna (Sattva/Rajas/Tamas inferred dynamically — "I can't get out of bed" = Tamas, "I'll destroy that project" = Rajas, "I forgive him" = Sattva growth); and Narrative Identity.

PART 4 — Indian Emotion Theory and the Gita

4A. Natyashastra. Bharata Muni's system: 9 rasas (Shringara/love, Hasya/comic, Karuna/compassion-sorrow, Raudra/fury, Vira/heroism, Bhayanaka/fear, Bibhatsa/disgust, Adbhuta/wonder, Shanta/peace, the ninth added post-Bharata); 8 sthayibhavas; 33 vyabhichari-bhavas (Nirveda, Glani, Shanka, Asuya, Mada, Shrama, Alasya, Dainya, Chinta, Moha, Smriti, Dhriti, Vrida, Chapalata, Harsha, Avega, Jadata, Garva, Vishada, Autsukya, Nidra, Apasmara, Supta, Vibodha, Amarsha, Avahittha, Ugrata, Mati, Vyadhi, Unmada, Marana, Trasa, Vitarka); 8 sattvika bhavas (Stambha/paralysis, Sveda/perspiration, Romanca/horripilation, Svarabheda/voice-break, Kampa/trembling, Vaivarnya/pallor, Ashru/tears, Pralaya/fainting) — the sattvikas, especially Svarabheda (voice break) and Kampa (tremor), are directly detectable in a voice-first interface via prosody. Total 49 bhavas. This system may be more relevant than Western taxonomies because it already integrates trigger (vibhava), expression (anubhava), transitory state (vyabhichari), and physiological tell (sattvika) into a single generative model — precisely the multi-layer structure Krishna AI needs. Map every rasa and vyabhichari-bhava onto the Part 1 ontology (e.g., Vishada→despondency/anticipatory grief; Nirveda→dispassion or, clinically, anhedonia; Chinta→rumination/anxiety; Glani→exhaustion/depletion).

4B. Gita emotional lexicon. Core Sanskrit terms with verse loci and commentary: Shoka (grief; 1.47 "shoka-samvigna-manasah," 2.11), Moha (delusion/attachment — Shankara reads Arjuna's Ch1 state as fundamentally moha masquerading as vairagya), Vishada (despondency, Ch1 title "Arjuna-vishada-yoga"), Nirveda (dispassion/world-weariness, 2.52), Vairagya (dispassion, 6.35 "abhyasena … vairagyena"), Kama/Krodha/Lobha (desire/anger/greed; the "three gates of hell," 16.21), Bhaya (fear), Shanti (peace, 2.66, 2.71), Shraddha (faith, 17.3), Bhakti, Prema, Sharanagati (18.66), Ananda, and awe (Ch11). Each term should carry Devanagari, IAST, Monier-Williams/Apte glosses, all verse occurrences, and the Shankara/Ramanuja/Madhva/modern readings.

4C. Arjuna's arc as healing blueprint. Ch1 vishada/grief/confusion — listen and witness; Ch2 the eternal soul (Sankhya) — reframe identity; Ch3 karma yoga — channel energy; Ch4 knowledge — offer framework; Ch5 renunciation/equanimity — model peace; Ch6 meditation and the restless mind — teach practice; Ch7–11 the divine and revelation — expand perspective (Ch11 cosmic form as existential expansion / a form of exposure); Ch12 bhakti — offer intimate relationship; Ch13–17 Kshetra-Kshetrajna, gunas, virtue — teach discernment; Ch18 complete surrender — commitment and liberation. Each chapter template names Arjuna's sthayibhava, the arising vyabhichari-bhavas, Krishna's response strategy, and the modern intervention parallel (reflective listening, cognitive restructuring, exposure, ACT-style commitment).

4D. Bhav system. Gaudiya Vaishnava five bhavas (progression grounded in Vishvanatha Chakravarti's Madhurya Kadambini stages shraddha→sadhu-sanga→bhajana-kriya→anartha-nivritti→nishtha→ruchi→asakti→bhava→prema): Shanta (neutral equanimity, for beginners), Dasya (servant, 18.73), Sakhya (friend, 11.44 direct address), Vatsalya (parental, for nurturing wounds), Madhurya (beloved, 9.34 manmana bhava, for the broken-hearted). Critical real-time distinction: viraha (sweet pain of divine separation, like the Gopis — advanced devotion, honour it) vs. vyatha (secular distress needing intervention). Detect via context: object of longing (divine vs. worldly), presence of devotional vocabulary and practice history, and whether the pain is oriented toward union with the sacred or loss of a worldly attachment.

4E. South Asian diaspora expression. Karasz et al. (2007), comparing South Asian (n=35) and European American (n=36) immigrant women, found the reverse of the stereotype for causal attribution: "43% of European American psychological problems included at least one physical cause, while only 4% of psychological problems in the South Asian group included at least one physical cause" — i.e., South Asians tended to situate distress in family/social/moral causes rather than internal-biological ones, even while presenting symptoms somatically to clinicians (weakness, cold, heart pain). Karasz et al. (2019, J Immigrant Minority Health 21(S1):7–14) document stigma, psycho-social-spiritual explanatory models, and under-treatment. Add lajja (shame, especially for men), "Log kya kahenge" (social-evaluative anxiety), parental-sacrifice guilt idioms, ABCD/model-minority/code-switching strain, and a preference for spiritual over psychological framing (Juthani 2004; Bhugra & Bhui 2001; Carpenter-Song et al. 2010). Hindu-specific spiritual bypassing uses karma/maya/detachment to avoid grief or anger — flag these as possible defences while still working with, not around, the spiritual frame.

PART 5 — Gita Mapping Database

Master cross-reference: each emotional state → primary verse (Sanskrit + IAST + translation) + appraisal-based "why this verse" (how it reframes the specific appraisal producing the emotion) + a Commentary Quartet (Shankara/Advaita, Ramanuja/Vishishtadvaita, Madhva/Dvaita, plus a modern voice — Prabhupada, Chinmayananda, Easwaran, or Vivekananda) + a pre-verse validation line mirroring the hidden emotion + 2–3 secondary verses. Anchor verses to annotate first: 2.14 (impermanence), 2.20 (eternal soul → grief/death/existential terror), 2.47 (duty without attachment → control anxiety), 2.48 (yoga as equanimity → overwhelm), 2.62–63 (the anger chain → reactivity), 3.27 (I am not the doer → ego deflation), 4.7–8 (I come when dharma declines → despair/meaninglessness), 6.5 (uplift the self by the self → self-blame), 6.34–35 (the restless mind → anxiety/scattered attention), 9.22 (I care for those who surrender → exhausted caregivers), 9.34 (fill your mind with Me → devotional longing), 10.20 (I am the Self in all beings → cosmic loneliness), 11.33 (you are the instrument → paralysis), 12.13–14 (the devotee of equanimity → developing sattva), 18.58 (by My grace you will overcome → hopelessness), 18.66 (abandon all dharmas and surrender → the end of one's rope). The Wisdom Graph tags every verse across 35+ dimensions (emotional states, needs, life stage, attachment, virtue/vice, decision type, identity theme, conflict type, guna, bhav mode, crisis level, conversation stage, modern-psychology parallel, etc.).

PART 6 — Conversation Architecture

3-Act Compassionate Arc: Act 1 — deep validation (name the unspoken feeling, not the spoken one); Act 2 — 1–2 Socratic listening questions (Miller & Rollnick MI; Overholser on Socratic method; the Gita's own question-before-teaching structure); Act 3 — the Gita's illumination (verse in narrative context, two commentary lenses in one sentence each, tied to the user's own words). Session arc: Safety → Understanding → Clarification → Meaning → Wisdom → Action/Surrender (karma yoga vs. jnana/bhakti release) → Hope. Readiness/timing: Prochaska & DiClemente stages of change (precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, maintenance) with MI matched to stage — validation-only in early/low-safety states; challenge only when readiness signals are present. Guru-Testing Paradox: Krishna's challenge in 2.2–3 ("this weakness does not befit you, Arjuna") worked because the alliance and Arjuna's readiness were established; comfort that enables avoidance calls for gentle challenge, but challenge in acute grief or crisis is harmful. Build the readiness layer with as much rigour as the verse-selection layer. 
