PART 7 — Memory System

Therapeutic memory tracks recurring fears (frequency, intensity trend, triggers, most-effective interventions), identity wounds, Shraddha→Prema progress, healing verses and evidence of impact, IFS Parts, breakthroughs and their precipitants, narrative themes, relationship patterns, and opening questions. Soul Graph = living per-user model (guna profile, karmic-pattern registry, chakra-domain map as metaphor not literal but diagnostically useful, devotional-stage tracker, wound register, belief map, bhav inclination, breakthrough log, verse-resonance map). Idiolect modelling — a per-user phrase→emotion probability map ("I'm cooked" = overwhelmed; "it's getting heavy" = approaching breakdown), register-shift detection (sudden brevity/increased hedging/topic avoidance as pre-breakdown signals), a question-effectiveness map, verse-resonance history, and faith-framing preference (Advaita vs. Bhakti; literal vs. metaphorical). This per-user layer is the differentiator over population-level NLP, which cannot know these idiosyncratic signals.

PART 8 — Crisis Protocol

Ground detection in the Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS) and the NLP suicide-detection literature: distinguish passive ideation from active planning from ambivalence; cover direct, indirect, veiled, and passive markers (first-person-singular density, anger words when discussing work/relationships, hopelessness/burden language). NLP can detect risk even without explicit disclosure (clinical-interview studies report AUC ≈ 0.76–0.92), but this is decision-support, not diagnosis. Routing: any active ideation or planning → immediate connection to human crisis services (e.g., the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline in the US and local equivalents), never handled by the AI alone. De-escalation language from DBT (Linehan 1993) and crisis-intervention research. Hard rules: never delay professional referral; never let the AI manage an active suicidal crisis alone; never apply spiritual bypassing (karma, detachment, "this is meant to be") to acute distress; follow established safe-messaging guidelines.

PART 9 — The Krishna Reasoning Layer

Encode how Krishna teaches, not just what — the pedagogic logic beneath each intervention, because the right verse at the wrong moment alienates rather than heals. The core per-turn decision: given this person, this emotional state, this readiness level, and this session stage, should the system CHALLENGE, COMFORT, QUESTION, stay SILENT, tell a STORY, use LOGIC (Sankhya for the intellectualiser who has blocked feeling with thought), use METAPHOR (Lakoff & Johnson conceptual-metaphor theory, for content that cannot be approached directly), invoke DUTY/DHARMA (svadharma for paralysis/avoidance), IMPERMANENCE (for clinging/grief), DETACHMENT (for outcome-anxiety/perfectionism — and never for fresh grief, early loss, or as spiritual bypass), DEVOTION (for loneliness/heartbreak/cosmic abandonment), PRACTICE (when insight has occurred but embodiment is missing — generate personalised tapasya), or ACTION (karma yoga when only movement remains). A Teaching-Style × Emotional-State matrix specifies, per cell: primary style, secondary style if the first is refused, verse category, specific linguistic approach, and what to avoid.

The deepest research question — can we model how wisdom changes a person? Draw on four literatures: (1) Mezirow's transformative learning — a "disorienting dilemma" triggers self-examination, critical reflection, exploration and planning of new roles, and reintegration (a 10-phase sequence, consensus phrasing); (2) insight/"aha-moment" research in psychotherapy; (3) post-traumatic growth — Tedeschi & Calhoun (1996, J. Traumatic Stress 9(3):455–471, DOI 10.1002/jts.2490090305), whose 21-item PTGI has five factors: New Possibilities, Relating to Others, Personal Strength, Spiritual Change, Appreciation of Life; and (4) the Gita's own theory of change, Shraddha (faith) → Viveka (discernment) → Vairagya (dispassion) → Abhyasa (practice) → Samadhi (integration). Each stage maps to a system behaviour: build faith through reliable attunement before offering discernment, and never push practice before insight has landed.

PART 10 — Evaluation Framework

Reject BLEU and generic chatbot benchmarks. Custom metrics: Understanding Accuracy (blind-annotator consensus on whether the underlying concern, not the stated one, was inferred), Question Quality (self-report + linguistic depth of the user's reply), Verse Fidelity (expert scholar panel across commentary traditions), Authenticity (faithful to the Gita's spirit vs. forcing a verse or bypassing), Emotional Resonance (post-session single item), Reflection Depth (linguistic complexity/self-reference/nuance increase across a session), Actionability (coded follow-up check-in), Longitudinal Growth (PERMA, MHQ, Meaning in Life Questionnaire [Steger et al. 2006], Self-Compassion Scale — administered monthly), Parts-Recognition Accuracy (AI-inferred vs. user retrospective self-report), and Timing Accuracy (expert flags of premature verse delivery). Each carries an instrument, cadence, minimum detectable change, and the Gita-aligned intervention when a dimension stagnates. Empathy operationalisation should follow Sharma, Miner, Atkins & Althoff (2020, EMNLP pp. 5263–5276, DOI 10.18653/v1/2020.emnlp-main.425), which decomposes empathy into emotional reactions, interpretations, and explorations — and found "stronger explorations get 47% more replies," a direct signal that inviting the user to say more, not delivering answers, drives engagement.

Recommendations

Stage 1 — Build the detection spine. Implement the emotion ontology (start with Shaver's 135 + GoEmotions' 27 + Parrott's tree, VAD-anchored via Warriner) and three engines: Emotion Analyzer, Appraisal Analyzer, Crisis Detector. Ship crisis routing first — it is the hard safety gate. Benchmarks: surface-emotion macro-F1 ≥ 0.50 (the GoEmotions BERT baseline is 0.46, so this is a modest, achievable bar); crisis recall ≥ 0.95 on held-out annotated data (recall prioritised over precision).

Stage 2 — Add the hidden layer. Defence-mechanism detector, IFS Parts decomposer, Bhav detector (viraha vs. vyatha), and the Soul Graph memory schema. Benchmarks: hidden-emotion inference validated against clinician annotation; Parts-recognition accuracy vs. user retrospective self-report ≥ 0.6 agreement.

Stage 3 — Wisdom reasoning + idiolect. Build the Krishna Reasoning Layer decision tree and the Teaching-Style × Emotional-State matrix; deploy per-user idiolect modelling and the Wisdom Graph tagging of all 700 verses. Benchmarks: expert-flagged premature-verse rate < 10%; Emotional Resonance ≥ 4/5; measurable emotional-granularity increase in the longitudinal cohort.

Thresholds that change the plan: if crisis recall cannot exceed ~0.95, narrow the product's scope and foreground human referral far more aggressively; if emotional-granularity gains do not appear by month 3 of longitudinal cohorts, revisit whether the conversation is actually teaching differentiation or merely labelling it; if Verse-Fidelity scores from the scholar panel diverge sharply across traditions for a given state, present the Commentary Quartet as explicitly plural rather than picking one reading.

Caveats
Effect sizes are modest and features are noisy. First-person-pronoun→depression links are small (r ≈ 0.13) and better read as general-distress markers; do not over-weight any single linguistic feature. GoEmotions inter-annotator agreement is low (mean κ ≈ 0.27) and classes are imbalanced (grief, relief, pride each < 1k examples), so fine-grained detection must be treated as probabilistic and always checked against session context.
The product is wisdom-based transformation, not therapy. Every genuine clinical need must route to human professionals; IFS/MI/DBT constructs inform design, but the AI must never present as a clinician or a crisis service.
Instrument-validity flags. The TOSCA shame/guilt scale is critiqued for confounding adaptive-guilt with maladaptive-shame; the PTGI five-factor model fits only adequately in confirmatory analyses and its factors are highly intercorrelated; Mezirow's 10 phases are consensus phrasing rather than a single verbatim source. The reversed somatisation finding in Karasz et al. (2007) — South Asians attributing distress to social/moral rather than physical causes even while presenting somatically — shows that "somatisation" is about presentation and help-seeking, not necessarily internal causal models; design accordingly.
Sanskrit interpretation is genuinely contested. Shankara (Advaita), Ramanuja (Vishishtadvaita), and Madhva (Dvaita) read the same verses to substantively different metaphysical conclusions; Vallabha (Shuddhadvaita), Nimbarka, and the Gaudiya line add further readings. The system must present the Commentary Quartet as plural and let the user's own faith-framing preference weight the emphasis. 
